faqs:
  - q: "Is Base64 considered encryption?"
    a: "No. Base64 is an encoding algorithm, not encryption. It provides zero security, confidential protection, or cryptographic secrecy. Anyone who receives a Base64 string can instantly decode it back to plain text without a key."
  - q: "What is the difference between standard Base64 and URL-safe Base64?"
    a: "Standard Base64 (RFC 4648 §4) uses '+' and '/' in its 64-character alphabet, which require percent-encoding when placed inside URLs or filenames. URL-safe Base64 (RFC 4648 §5) substitutes '-' for '+' and '_' for '/', eliminating URL delimiter conflicts."
  - q: "Does this encoder safely handle UTF-8 characters and emojis?"
    a: "Yes. Standard browser btoa() fails on characters beyond the Latin-1 range. Our tool converts all text through native UTF-8 byte encoders, guaranteeing full fidelity for accented letters, Asian alphabets, and emojis (e.g. 🚀)."
  - q: "Is my text transmitted to any server?"
    a: "No. All encoding and decoding runs 100% locally in your browser memory. We never transmit, log, or store any data."
  - q: "Why does Base64 output end with '=' equal signs?"
    a: "The '=' symbol is padding. Base64 processes data in 3-byte chunks (24 bits) into four 6-bit characters. If the input byte length is not divisible by 3, one or two '=' characters pad the final block."
sources:
