    <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
    <section style="margin: 2.5rem 0;" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Base64 Pitfalls: Encoding vs Encryption</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Confusing Encoding with Encryption:</strong> Base64 is not a cipher. It contains no secret key, initialization vector, or computational hardness. Anyone can decode Base64 in milliseconds. For security, apply AES-GCM or ChaCha20 encryption before Base64 encoding.</li>
        <li><strong>Surrogate Pair Corruption with btoa():</strong> The native JavaScript <code>window.btoa()</code> function assumes Latin-1 encoding (code points 0–255). Passing characters like emojis or accented vowels throws an uncaught DOMException. Proper implementations always encode strings to UTF-8 byte arrays first.</li>
        <li><strong>Unpadded String Truncation:</strong> Some web frameworks strip trailing <code>=</code> padding characters. A robust decoder must normalize padding length to a multiple of 4 before processing to prevent decoding errors.</li>
      </ul>
